<p>
    <strong>Core Gameplan:</strong> Game 1 is a race to see who lands the <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
    > plus <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
    > lock first. Get a <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
    > down early—you need it to answer their combo the moment they try to go
    off.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Sideboard Strategy:</strong> The matchup changes a lot after
    board. A lot of players reflexively bring in <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
    > or <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
    >, but <strong>Angels are terrible in the mirror</strong>—they're slow,
    and they just get exiled by an opposing <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
    > or picked off by <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
        >Swords to Plowshares</a
    >.
</p>
<p>
    The real post-board MVPs are <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
    > and <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
    >. Abeyance protects your own <a
        href="#"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
    > or blows up their setup turn, and Seal gives you instant-speed answers
    to their enchantments. Leave every Exalted Angel and Meddling Mage in
    the board.
</p>
